description.narrative:
Oxfam and its partner (AJOAGO) propose to support the response through a range of activities to improve access to safe water, hygiene practices, community engagement, case surveillance and promoting access to improved sanitation. Since 2001, AJOAGO  is involved in successfully preparedness and response, improving water, sanitation and hygiene services, food security and other programmes in rural communities of Mozambique. As a result of cyclone Eloise Response that hit the district of Buzi in Sofala Province, AJOAGO, is involved in the  emergency response in Buzi.
Below are the proposed main objectives for a period of 3 months to combat cholera in thetarget area of Buzi district in Sofala Province:
1. Increased knowledge and awareness amongst 55,000 people in high-risk locations of Búzi district.
2. Improved access to safe and adequate water, sanitation facilities and hygiene promotion for women, men, boys and girls in affected communities.
3. Increased case surveillance to mitigate the spread of Cholera in affected communities.
4. Increased knowledge and awareness among 55, 000 people (29,150 Females and 25,850 males) on protection, safeguarding and safety in targeted communities.
The above objectives shall be achieved through the following summarized activities.
1. Train 20 Community health Volunteers to conduct door to door hygiene promotion on key message for cholera prevention and reach our 55,000 people;
2. Distribute 1,000 Cholera Response kits (buckets, oral rehydration salts, latrine disinfection sprayers, soap, water treatment chemical, IEC materials, etc.) to affected and hotspot community;
3. Support the establishment of rapid response teams for cholera contact tracing and case investigation and response including targeted distribution of NFIs;
4. Rehabilitation/repair of 5 broken public water points;
5. Train 15 school champions on hygiene promotion in schools;
6. Install 20 handwashing stations at public places;
7. Training of 5 Water point Committees
8. Community awareness raising on Safeguarding and Protection from Sexual Exploitation and Abuse.
9. Conduct mass media campaigns through roadshows and radio sessions
10. Distribution of dignity kits to women and girls
11. Water quality monitoring
12. Participate in the celebrations of the World Water Day as part of the awareness campaigns on water preservation, the correct and rational use of water and on the promotion of good hygiene practices.
13. Conduct PDMs and community feedback.

participating-org.narrative:
AJOAGO was founded in January 2001 in Govuro District, Inhambane Province.

AJOAGO is a legal person of private law, without profit, fostering socio-economic and community development vulnerable rural, with legal personality and administrative autonomy,
financialand asset.

AJOAGO not pursue ends that have any identification political - party, ethnic, tribal, regional or social exclusion by their various forms
The AJOAGO aims to contribute to the eradication of absolute poverty and the factors inherent.

AJOAGO aims:
Contribute to reducing disaster risk strengthening the processes of prevention, response and mitigation of vulnerable communities;
Contribute to improving the livelihoods of poor people;
Fostering capacity of the institutions andfor local adaptation and mitigate the climatic diversity.
Promote gender equity, right to life and dignity on HIV - AIDS.
Advocate the government to make decisions that lead to change and / or improvement governance, at all levels, so to benefit citizens;
Develop individual skills and institutional toanswer organizational growthneeds.

Main interventions areas:
Sustainable Livelihoods;
Disaster Risk Reduction;
Adaptation and Mitigation climate change;
Children and Youth.
Gender and HIV - AIDS and;
Advocacy and Governance.
